## Why I Built This
Client-facing and executive communications were going through multiple revision rounds with inconsistent results. The core problem was format mismatch — the same raw draft could become an email, a Teams update, or an MoM, and the wrong structure destroyed credibility. This skill detects the output type first and applies a type-specific template, producing a send-ready version in one pass.

## Methodology
The skill runs a five-step pipeline: detect output type, ask a maximum of three clarifying questions (only what would materially change the output), build using a type-specific template, run a silent quality checklist, then append a confidence signal. The zero-information-loss rule is the hardest constraint — every fact, figure, date, and ask from the raw input must appear in the output — and is checked explicitly before finalising.

## How to Use
Trigger: "Clean up this email" / "Rewrite this" / paste raw text with any instruction to improve it
Inputs needed: Raw draft (email, notes, message thread, or brain-dump); recipient name and title if not in the draft; the key ask or action needed if not obvious.

## What Good Output Looks Like
A message in the correct format for its type, with a specific subject line, a one-sentence opening hook that tells the recipient exactly what this is and what's needed, and a single unambiguous closing ask. Ends with ✅ Ready to send. or ⚠ Review recommended — [specific reason].

## Known Failure Modes
Type detection can misread an internal email as external if the recipient context is ambiguous — always verify the output type stated at the top. Long raw drafts with multiple asks can collapse into one; spot-check that secondary points haven't been dropped.
